-- title:
--   Independence of the entire sampled future Lévy path from every finite-prefix independence law
-- statement:
--   For a spectrally negative Lévy process X, a deterministic past time s and an arbitrary countable schedule τ:Nat→nonnegative times after s, suppose that, for every finite prefix length n, the sigma algebra jointly generated by the first n future increments X_(s+τ_i)−X_s (0≤i<n) is independent of the full history filtration F_s. Then the sigma algebra of the ENTIRE countably sampled future increment path (i↦X_(s+τ_i)−X_s) is independent of F_s. This proves the exact measure-theoretic infinite-dimensional closure, not merely coordinatewise independence. It uses the exact-commit Mathlib comap_process_pi sigma identity and indep_iSup_of_monotone, and proves the monotonicity, ambient measurability and equality of the finite prefix supremum with the full process sigma algebra. Finite-prefix joint independence must still be supplied via the Lévy independent-increment induction, and extension from a dense sample to all continuous times uses the càdlàg right-continuity assumptions separately. This result therefore closes a substantial but clearly delimited path-space argument.
-- source:
--   Original Lévy process adaptedness and IsProbabilityMeasure; pinned Mathlib MeasurableSpace.comap_process_pi and ProbabilityTheory.indep_iSup_of_monotone at Mathlib revision 0df444a360eaa60ab8c11dca51a86af692955474.

